course: biology
lesson: 314
title: Bats of the world: flying foxes, vampire bats, the bumblebee bat and tent-makers
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Bats are the only mammals that truly fly. Their wing is a hand: four very long fingers hold up a thin skin, and a short clawed thumb sticks out at the top.
explain: There are more than 1,400 kinds, about one in five of all mammal kinds, from flying foxes with wings as wide as a grown-up's arms to a bat as light as two paper clips.
explain: Some eat insects, some fruit, some nectar, and three kinds drink blood. Many plants need bats to carry their pollen or spread their seeds.
explain: Hang from a climbing frame by your knees and you get tired in a minute. A bat sleeps all day upside down without effort, because its toes lock shut on their own.
fact: Flying foxes are big fruit bats with wings up to about 1.5 metres across; they find fruit and nectar with good eyes and noses, not echoes, and spread seeds and pollen across forests.
fact: Flying foxes roost together by day in noisy camps of thousands, where many eyes watch for danger; in heatwaves many can die, and trained volunteers try to help them.
fact: There are more than 1,400 kinds of bat, about one in five mammal kinds; only rodents have more kinds.
fact: A bat's wing is a hand: four long fingers hold up the skin and a short clawed thumb sticks out; the skin stretches between the fingers, the body and the legs, and the name Chiroptera means hand-wing.
fact: Three kinds of vampire bat live in Central and South America; they make a small cut with razor-sharp teeth and lap up the blood, mostly from cattle, horses and birds, and do not suck it.
fact: Vampire bat spit stops blood going thick and sticky, so the cut keeps flowing, and scientists have studied it to make new medicines.
fact: A vampire bat that misses its meal for about two nights can starve; roost-mates that fed bring up blood to share, often with bats that shared with them before.
fact: Vampire bats can walk, run and hop on all fours, creeping up on a sleeping animal on foot, while most bats are clumsy on the ground.
fact: Kitti's hog-nosed bat, the bumblebee bat of Thailand and Myanmar, weighs about 2 grams and is one of the smallest mammals, along with the Etruscan shrew.
fact: Tent-making bats bite along a big leaf so it folds down into a tent that keeps off rain and hides them; Honduran white bats sleep under leaves where green light makes their white fur look green.
fact: Bats hang by their feet with tendons that lock their toes shut, so hanging takes no effort, and they drop into the air to take off because their legs are too weak to run into flight.
fact: Wild bananas, durians and agave plants are pollinated by bats; bat flowers are pale, strong-smelling and open at night, and one small nectar bat has a tongue about one and a half times as long as its body.
exercise: How do flying foxes find their food? | With good eyes and noses, not echoes | Only by echoes, as bats are blind | By following bees | By listening for falling fruit only
exercise: How wide can a flying fox's wings be? | Up to about 1.5 metres | About 15 centimetres, like a sparrow | About 5 metres, like a small plane | About 1.5 centimetres, like a fly
exercise: How do flying foxes help forests? | They spread seeds and pollen, planting new trees | They eat all the leaves off the tall trees so light gets in | They dig holes for roots | They chase away all the birds
exercise: Why do flying foxes live in big camps? | Many eyes can watch for danger | It is too cold to sleep alone | They have to share one tree each | They are scared of the dark
exercise: About what share of all mammal kinds are bats? | About one in five | About one in a hundred | About half of them | Nearly all of them
exercise: Which mammal group has more kinds than bats? | Rodents | Whales and dolphins | Wild cats | Monkeys and apes
exercise: How many long fingers hold up a bat's wing? | Four, plus a short clawed thumb | Two, plus a long thumb | Ten, like our two hands | None, a bat's wing has feathers
exercise: What does the bat name Chiroptera mean? | Hand-wing | Night-mouse | Blind-flyer | Cave-sleeper
exercise: How does a vampire bat feed? | It makes a small cut and laps up the blood | It sucks blood through hollow fangs | It drinks blood from people's necks every night | It eats the whole animal
exercise: Where do vampire bats live? | Central and South America | Transylvania, in the mountains of Europe | All over Britain | Only in Australia
exercise: What do vampire bats do when a roost-mate has missed its meal? | Bring up blood to share with it | Chase it out of the roost | Let it starve to make more room | Give it fruit instead
exercise: How does a vampire bat get close to a sleeping animal? | It lands nearby and creeps up on all fours | It dives straight onto its neck from high up | It hangs from the animal's ear | It digs a tunnel under it
exercise: About how much does a bumblebee bat weigh? | About 2 grams, like two paper clips | About 2 kilograms, like a bag of sugar | About 200 grams, like an apple | About 20 grams, like a big egg
exercise: How do tent-making bats make a shelter? | They bite along a big leaf so it folds down | They weave dry grass into a round ball, like a nest | They dig a deep hole into the trunk of a dead tree | They borrow old nests that birds have left behind
exercise: Why does hanging all day not tire a bat? | Tendons lock its toes shut without effort | It holds on with its sharp little teeth all day | It sleeps standing on the branch | It only hangs for a few minutes at a time
exercise: Why do bats drop to take off? | Their legs are too weak to run into flight | They are too heavy to flap their wings from the ground | They cannot see the sky | They must touch the ground first
exercise: Which plants are pollinated by bats? | Wild bananas, durians and agave | Grass, wheat and the tall oak trees | Pine trees, ferns and the big conifers | Seaweed, moss and the lichens on rocks
test: Count them: a bat wing has 4 long fingers and a thumb. How many long wing fingers does one bat have? | 8 | 4 | 10 | 5
test: Count them: 3 bats hang upside down by their feet. How many feet are holding on? | 6 | 3 | 12 | 9
test: In a Dracula cartoon a vampire bat bites a man's neck and sucks out his blood. What is the truth? | Real vampire bats make a small cut on cattle or birds and lap the blood | Real vampire bats suck people dry every night through two hollow fangs | Real vampire bats turn people into bats | Real vampire bats only eat fruit
test: «u cant tell me bats are friends, they r creepy blood suckers» What does science show about vampire bats? | They share food with roost-mates, often ones that shared with them | They never help each other at all | They steal food from each other every night, even from their own babies | Only flying foxes have friends
test: Count it: a bumblebee bat weighs about 2 grams and a big flying fox about 1 kilogram. About how many bumblebee bats weigh the same as one flying fox? | About 500 | About 50 | About 5 | About 5,000
test: What would happen to wild banana plants in a forest if all the nectar bats vanished? | Fewer of their flowers would be pollinated, so fewer seeds | They would grow twice as fast | Nothing, bananas need only rain | They would turn into trees with no fruit at all
test: A flower is pale, smells strong and opens only at night. Which animal most likely pollinates it? | A bat | A butterfly | A hummingbird | A honey bee
test: Why would a bat with white fur hide well under a leaf? | Light through the leaf makes the white fur look green | White fur is invisible in bright sunlight, like glass | Snakes cannot see the colour white | The leaf paints the fur every day
test: «bats are just flying mice innit» What is right? | No, bats are a separate group of mammals, not mice | Yes, bats are just mice that grew wings when they got old | Yes, mice turn into bats when old | No, bats are birds with fur
test: What would happen if a bat's toes needed muscles to stay shut? | It would get tired hanging, like you on a climbing frame | It could hang even longer | It would fly higher | It would have to sleep standing up on the branch like a bird
test: «the vet says vampire bat spit might help ppl, how» What is the answer? | It stops blood going thick and sticky, which scientists study for medicines | It cures toothache in one day | It makes people grow wings | It turns blood into water
exam: Identify the structure that supports the membrane of a bat's wing. | Four elongated digits of the forelimb, with the thumb remaining short and clawed | A single elongated bone extending from the shoulder | A series of feathers anchored in the skin | Cartilaginous rods unconnected with the hand
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the feeding of vampire bats. | They inflict a small incision and lap the blood, chiefly of cattle, horses and birds | They extract blood by suction through hollow canine teeth | They feed principally upon human beings in Europe | They feed exclusively upon the nectar of night-flowering plants of the tropical forests
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises Kitti's hog-nosed bat? | It weighs approximately two grams and ranks among the smallest of mammals | It weighs approximately two kilograms and ranks among the largest of bats | It is a fruit bat with wings exceeding one metre in span | It is a species of bird mistakenly classified as a bat
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a bat expends little effort when hanging inverted. | Tendons lock the toes in a closed position without muscular effort | The bat adheres to surfaces by means of a sticky secretion | The bat grips continuously with its teeth | The bat's weight is supported by its folded wings
